Ansible Variable Precedence (from lowest to highest)
Here’s the simplified order (lowest priority first):

Role defaults

Defined in defaults/main.yml inside a role.

Lowest priority, meant as safe fallbacks.

Inventory variables

From group_vars and host_vars.

Group variables apply to all hosts in that group.

Host variables override group variables.

Playbook variables

Defined directly in the playbook under vars:.

Higher priority than inventory.

Include/import vars

Variables loaded via include_vars or vars_files.

Extra vars from registered facts

Facts gathered from hosts (ansible_facts).

Can override inventory/playbook vars.


Block vars

Variables defined inside a block: section.

Task vars

Variables defined directly in a task.

Role vars

Defined in vars/main.yml inside a role.

Higher priority than defaults.

Include/import role vars

Variables passed when including/importing roles.

Command-line -e (extra-vars)

Highest priority. Always wins, no matter what.

--------------------------
Suppose you define http_port in multiple places:

defaults/main.yml → http_port: 80

group_vars/webservers.yml → http_port: 8080

Playbook vars: → http_port: 9090

Run command → ansible-playbook site.yml -e "http_port=10000"

👉 The final value used will be 10000, because extra-vars override everything.

---------------------

Lowest priority → Role defaults
                  Inventory (group_vars, host_vars)
                  Playbook vars
                  Include/import vars
                  Facts
                  Block vars
                  Task vars
                  Role vars
                  Include/import role vars
Highest priority → Extra-vars (-e)
